[{:db/id "q-350549/field-1/350be8298d601fdf1f4014856a933ef92843a746d7243f4c549f50d3b5d2e557" :answer/id #uuid "22fee74e-9c12-5e28-9bdf-f4a0eea9568b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4t(1+t^2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350549/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "09e5b403-6604-5960-8130-feded318602a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350549/field-1/350be8298d601fdf1f4014856a933ef92843a746d7243f4c549f50d3b5d2e557" :answer-field/choices ["q-350549/field-1/350be8298d601fdf1f4014856a933ef92843a746d7243f4c549f50d3b5d2e557"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350549" :question/id #uuid "259fa305-7a07-58cd-bad6-735f377f064d" :question/math-academy-id "q-350549" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{2}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=4t(1+{t}^{2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-350549/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350549"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352879/field-1/1669f11a0d5569b36559886b1c30c4c147bc2e0dadc9c2ea8daead62b29a9a5a" :answer/id #uuid "4c71fb3b-f2a5-5244-a635-82a4a8ba4e51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}e^{-t}+e^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352879/field-1/d302c222f62c68b163628f6cd3472c6435d4e5aa69dba43b5675c80b2a60edcd" :answer/id #uuid "dd6f9b8c-e5cb-5598-97d1-01af89ed124e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{-t}+e^{-2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352879/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ada744c-41c1-5a29-8b0c-17d0fdb4d4bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352879/field-1/1669f11a0d5569b36559886b1c30c4c147bc2e0dadc9c2ea8daead62b29a9a5a" :answer-field/choices ["q-352879/field-1/1669f11a0d5569b36559886b1c30c4c147bc2e0dadc9c2ea8daead62b29a9a5a" "q-352879/field-1/d302c222f62c68b163628f6cd3472c6435d4e5aa69dba43b5675c80b2a60edcd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352879" :question/id #uuid "13045f32-cece-5ee0-ad5f-5c153a7a3a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-352879" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{3}] & =3({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-t}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{e}^{-t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{-2t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{-t}(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-352879/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352879"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332944/field-1/982fc2a253b2a13d824573e57ad976d43065eb9f2f7e4e980b83042f1988faf5" :answer/id #uuid "c56f45b8-10dd-51a2-b414-43379bcbec60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6t^2(1+t^3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228085 :answer-field/correct "q-332944/field-1/982fc2a253b2a13d824573e57ad976d43065eb9f2f7e4e980b83042f1988faf5" :answer-field/choices ["q-332944/field-1/982fc2a253b2a13d824573e57ad976d43065eb9f2f7e4e980b83042f1988faf5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332944"] :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{3}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334969/field-1/c209e197563cf2eb10a3858ad698a319900f43acdb7d60995557846227af3f3b" :answer/id #uuid "a291c3d1-ccb8-5c81-abe2-1cc060ebdc5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\cos(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334969/field-1/3f149c205f88dc69e184d9eb1f315726544477aa04303c16de14741a45797847" :answer/id #uuid "44b92202-2503-5b5d-be00-fba7f4e2f445" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+\\cos(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334969/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba15556f-19c5-54ff-a8cc-a19c1dd83e84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334969/field-1/c209e197563cf2eb10a3858ad698a319900f43acdb7d60995557846227af3f3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-334969/field-1/c209e197563cf2eb10a3858ad698a319900f43acdb7d60995557846227af3f3b" "q-334969/field-1/3f149c205f88dc69e184d9eb1f315726544477aa04303c16de14741a45797847"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334969" :question/id #uuid "dc6ca2a6-f6a7-5e41-ae76-99e53e294df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334969" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,x{|}_{-2}^{3}-{x}^{2}{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334969/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334969"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334931/field-1/bd5daa981a737b36e92483072a3d9026e52c6b99cc8c0ddb78bcbe78f1caf4f7" :answer/id #uuid "05319df9-0f11-5158-a81b-993d0334a33a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48t^2(4t^3-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334931/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "15e1362f-3c17-54e1-bfb8-241ac3d70534" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334931/field-1/bd5daa981a737b36e92483072a3d9026e52c6b99cc8c0ddb78bcbe78f1caf4f7" :answer-field/choices ["q-334931/field-1/bd5daa981a737b36e92483072a3d9026e52c6b99cc8c0ddb78bcbe78f1caf4f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334931" :question/id #uuid "62383116-90e1-54f6-a3e6-be20ccf80a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-334931" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x-4{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot (-12{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2}-4{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-8{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-24{t}^{2}(2-8{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =48{t}^{2}(4{t}^{3}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334931/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334931"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335254/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "a291cc29-6539-5e0e-a273-38f95629afc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335254/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "28f2af23-51cf-5201-b2a6-5cab51067938" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335254/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "dc2e51d1-2c12-59d4-a990-faed7dcb3bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335254/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "4388d650-bb8a-528f-83b1-0c2c13058ffb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335254/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "8785fc80-c16a-5f0a-aa6b-1aa3abf9e635" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335254/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9473a1-88e9-535f-ae1a-c4793a20bedb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335254/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-335254/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335254/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-335254/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-335254/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-335254/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335254" :question/id #uuid "ea250752-4cd0-5e27-8e08-643028b0da10" :question/math-academy-id "q-335254" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335254/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335254"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335246/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "5ccd1448-2d96-5b02-9851-81802a829220" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335246/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b59a4e00-f2a6-5893-8b15-a916a3e4c062" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335246/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "ce29aa53-6f74-5d83-ab7a-5ab1a5ecd6d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335246/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "de5f6e3f-f160-5f20-b543-82cdf56636d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335246/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5ab3f82a-594c-5659-bdbf-7d2a4ee4e216" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335246/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1156a224-821a-58c6-98e0-870accccf934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335246/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-335246/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335246/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-335246/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-335246/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-335246/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335246" :question/id #uuid "11579158-b44f-5ea4-9087-c8b32428e69f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335246" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{1}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2tx}{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{1+x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊂(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{1}{(1+x+t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335246/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335246"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335247/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "869f9e34-00e3-5e66-8361-3a5db185d1d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335247/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "72ef936d-4929-5b14-b256-76ff1b5efb6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335247/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "4dbaa03e-88bf-5d85-9cda-12ee90f9bfb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335247/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "6f5629e6-d958-5a8e-8c2b-b77a15f25f1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335247/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "4ba7bac5-9c09-59dc-a0b1-b8274e5e8825" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335247/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f539163-fa77-5396-898b-2e48d80e3e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335247/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-335247/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335247/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-335247/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-335247/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-335247/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335247" :question/id #uuid "2dd6f11c-4839-54cb-8c9d-7c7da33b80c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335247" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335247/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335247"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335251/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "67d4f15c-1970-5c8f-9633-9d0d2238c0a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335251/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "fc69be5c-c48c-5ba0-97b7-1eb82db7b44c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335251/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "43d32bc2-4073-5381-b98a-567bc2a48223" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335251/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "55f9ef50-b404-5db7-8f65-687d158f834f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335251/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "f9ef9151-5076-5e05-b513-372963712e9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335251/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c173abd-1245-5684-a5f3-018c5be6619b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335251/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-335251/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-335251/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-335251/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-335251/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-335251/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335251" :question/id #uuid "2025eb0b-c186-520b-9049-e64b856c84a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335251" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (-1,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (1,2)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (-1,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since $1+tx>0$). Computing the  partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(1+tx)(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (1,2)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,2)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (1,2).$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{3}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335251/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335251"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335249/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b849b6cb-9829-5c72-828a-8e89e25d9863" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "6d39ea14-d126-54e0-b74b-464e07d2fa6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335249/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "468220dd-d77a-533d-90ed-6e6bf0c423a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335249/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "939f903b-4635-58cb-b6ca-02fbb249e259" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335249/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "281793a3-25bc-55d5-936e-acea625cd39b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335249/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "46c86840-bb8a-5e86-8a45-74603cc808cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-335249/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-335249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-335249/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335249/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-335249/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335249" :question/id #uuid "594e1f73-6f7b-5ca0-a98d-c1f0d5cf4465" :question/math-academy-id "q-335249" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335249/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335249"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21649"] :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21732"] :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(x,t) = t\\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_1$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\nf_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1),$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\nf_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_3$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
